Abstract
Asteroseismology can provide constraints on the stellar properties of solar analogs 16 Cyg A and B
Properties of each star derived independently using AMP, giving a common age (6.8±0.4 Gyr) and initial composition

1 Introduction
Solar analogs are comparable to recent past and near future of the Sun
Long orbital period means no dynamical constraints on masses
2 stars modelled independently using first 3 months of Kepler data

2 Data analysis
High quality oscillation spectra show octuple (l=3) modes
Estimates of observed mode frequencies from peak-bagging techniques (maximum-likelihood estimation and MCMC)
Comparison between results different methods led to a list of robust modes

3 Asteroseismic modelling
Inputs for stellar modelling are 46/41 frequencies for 16 Cyg A/B, and spectroscopic properties (effective temp, log of surface gravity, metallicity)
Effective temps used to get bolometric corrections + extinction estimates, then derive luminosity constraints
Optimal models from AMP are a good match to observational data
Comparing different models of stellar evolution gives estimates of systematic uncertainty

4 Results and discussion
Adopted stellar properties after accounting for model dependence show that the stars share a common age and initial composition as expected for a binary system - asteroseismic inference shown to be reliable
More data may reveal rotational splitting of non-radial modes, or deviations in the small frequency spacings
